const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Do solar security cameras work on cloudy days in Seattle?",
    answer: "Yes, at reduced output. Solar panels still produce power under cloud, just less of it. NASA satellite data for 2001-2020 puts average December sunlight on flat ground around Seattle at 0.85 kWh per square meter per day, about half the clear-sky figure and about a seventh of July's. That's why a winter-ready solar camera relies on a battery to carry it through gray stretches and on a generator or shore power for the longest ones."
  },
  {
    question: "How long can a solar camera trailer run without sun?",
    answer: "It depends on usable battery capacity and on how much power the cameras, infrared lights, recorder and modem draw. Ask the vendor how many days the exact configuration runs with no sun at its December load, with infrared on all night, and what recharges it after that. For a Puget Sound winter, a good answer includes a generator or shore power, not only a bigger battery."
  },
  {
    question: "Will a solar surveillance trailer keep recording during a power outage?",
    answer: "Yes. A solar trailer runs on its own panels and battery, so a utility outage doesn't shut it down. That matters here: on September 25, 2026, Puget Sound Energy had 51,235 customers without power at 5:30 p.m., mostly around Olympia. Cameras running off a jobsite's temporary power stop when the grid does unless they have their own battery backup. Live view and alerts still depend on the cellular network staying up."
  },
  {
    question: "What happens to the footage if cell service goes down?",
    answer: "The recorder on the trailer keeps recording as long as the trailer has power. What you lose during a cellular outage is live view and alerts, until the connection comes back. After a storm, check the trailer and export any clips you need before the recorder's retention window loops over them. CCTV Trailer units keep two weeks of motion-only recording by default."
  },
  {
    question: "Does cold weather affect solar camera batteries in Western Washington?",
    answer: "It affects charging more than running. Lithium iron phosphate batteries shouldn't be charged when they're cold: Battle Born puts the limit at about 25 degrees F without internal heating, and Victron's Smart lithium batteries stop charging below 41 degrees F. By NOAA's 1991-2020 normals, Olympia averages 77 nights a year at or below freezing and Sea-Tac 25. On a cold morning an unheated battery may not accept a charge until it warms up."
  },
  {
    question: "Where should I park a solar camera trailer on a winter jobsite?",
    answer: "Where it has open sky to the south and a clear view of what you're protecting. At noon on December 21 the sun is only about 19 degrees above the horizon in Seattle, so a 100-foot fir on the south side throws a shadow roughly 290 feet long. Pick firm, drained ground that a truck can still reach after weeks of rain, and keep the trailer out from under trees that could come down in a windstorm."
  },
  {
    question: "How much does it cost to rent a solar surveillance trailer for the winter?",
    answer: "Pricing depends on rental length, site and configuration, so every job gets its own quote. For a project that runs through the winter, a monthly rental usually makes the most sense. Call CCTV Trailer at (253) 683-2288 or request a quote. Professional delivery and setup are available, and every rental includes equipment training and 24/7 technical support."
  },
  {
    question: "Do you deliver surveillance trailers to Olympia?",
    answer: "Yes. CCTV Trailer is based in Tacoma and serves Thurston County, including Olympia, along with Pierce, King, Snohomish and Kitsap counties. Call (253) 683-2288 to check availability for your site and dates. For a winter job, we can talk through where the trailer should sit and how it will stay powered before it ever leaves the yard."
  }
];

