---

<Layout
  title="Do Solar Security Cameras Work in a Seattle Winter? (Jobsite Guide) | CCTV Trailer"
  description="Do solar security cameras work in a Seattle winter? What short days, cloud, cold and storm outages do to a jobsite trailer in Tacoma, Seattle or Olympia."
  schema={schema}>

  <Header currentPath="/blog" showEmergencyBar={true} />
  <Analytics />
  <BreadcrumbSchema items={breadcrumbs} />
  <SpeakableSchema sections={speakableSections} />
  <FAQSchema items={faqs} />

  <article>
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <section class="hero-section-blog relative py-20 px-6 text-white">
      <div class="hero-overlay">
        <LazyImage src="/images/solar-winter-hero.webp" alt="A solar-powered CCTV Trailer mobile surveillance unit parked beside the chain-link fence of a Tacoma-area construction site at dusk on a rainy December afternoon, with a framed building, an excavator and stacked lumber behind it"
          class="w-full h-full object-cover" width="1920" height="1080" fetchPriority="high" loading="eager" />
      </div>
      <div class="absolute inset-0 z-[3] bg-gradient-to-r from-black/70 to-black/40"></div>
      <div class="container mx-auto max-w-4xl relative z-10">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-4">
          Do Solar Security Cameras Work in a Seattle Winter? A Jobsite Guide for Tacoma, Seattle and Olympia
        </h1>
        <div class="flex items-center text-lg mb-8">
          <time datetime="2026-10-09">October 9, 2026</time>
          <span class="mx-4">&bull;</span>
          <span>18 min read</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
          On Friday, September 25, an early-season windstorm came through the South Sound. Olympia recorded a 54 mph gust, and at 5:30 that afternoon Puget Sound Energy still had 51,235 customers without power, most of them around Olympia, Tumwater, Lacey and Yelm. Tacoma Public Utilities had another 2,389. Any jobsite camera running off a temporary power pole in those neighborhoods lost power along with everything else, unless it had a battery of its own. Clocks fall back on November 1, and by December 21 Seattle gets 8 hours and 25 minutes of daylight. So it's fair to ask whether solar security cameras actually keep working through a Seattle winter. They can. This guide covers what decides it: short days and a low sun, the fifth gray day in a row, freezing mornings, cell outages and where to park the trailer, plus a winter checklist for the rest of the site.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Quick Answer Section -->
    <QuickAnswer
      question="Do solar security cameras work through a Seattle, Tacoma or Olympia winter?"
      answer="Yes, if the whole power setup is built for December rather than July. Around Seattle, a flat surface gets about 0.85 kWh of sunlight per square meter on an average December day, roughly a seventh of July, and daylight drops to about 8.5 hours. A battery carries the cameras through long nights and gray stretches, and a generator or shore power covers the longest ones. Park the trailer clear of tree shade, keep the panels clean, and know what a cell outage does: the trailer keeps recording, but live view and alerts wait for the connection to come back."
    />

    <!-- Main Content -->
    <section class="py-16">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-6 max-w-4xl">
        <div class="blog-content prose prose-lg max-w-none">

          <!-- Stats Section -->
          <div class="mb-12">
            <StatBox
              layout="grid"
              stats={[
                {
                  value: "8 h 25 min",
