          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">1. Short days, a low sun and a lot of cloud</h2>

            <p>
              Start with the daylight. By the <a href="https://aa.usno.navy.mil/calculated/rstt/oneday?date=2026-12-21&lat=47.61&lon=-122.33&label=Seattle%2C+WA&tz=8.00&tz_sign=-1&tz_label=false&dst=true&submit=Get+Data" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">U.S. Naval Observatory's tables</a>, the sun rises in Seattle at 7:55 a.m. on December 21, 2026 and sets at 4:20 p.m. That's 8 hours and 25 minutes of daylight, against 15 hours and 59 minutes on June 21. Tacoma and Olympia, a little farther south, get a few more minutes in December: 8 hours 28 minutes and 8 hours 30 minutes. Daylight saving time ends at 2 a.m. on November 1, <a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/popular-links/daylight-saving-time-dst" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">per NIST</a>, and on Monday, November 2, the Seattle sunset is at 4:50 p.m. From about November 17 until the first days of January, the sun is down in Seattle before 4:30 p.m.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              The sun is also low. At solar noon on December 21 it stands about 19 degrees above the southern horizon in Seattle, by the Naval Observatory's numbers. In June it's about 66 degrees. Light coming in that low spreads thinner across the ground and travels through more atmosphere on the way. It also throws long shadows, which decides where a trailer should park (section 4).
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              Then there's the cloud. NASA's <a href="https://power.larc.nasa.gov/api/temporal/climatology/point?parameters=ALLSKY_SFC_SW_DWN,CLRSKY_SFC_SW_DWN&community=RE&longitude=-122.33&latitude=47.61&format=JSON" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">POWER climate data</a>, built from satellite measurements for 2001-2020, puts the average daily sunlight reaching flat ground around Seattle at 0.85 kWh per square meter in December and 6.33 in July. December gets about a seventh of July. On a clear December day the same spot would get about 1.66, so cloud takes roughly half of what little December sunlight there is. NASA's data doesn't separate Olympia from Seattle at this resolution, so the numbers are the same for both.
            </p>

            <!-- Chart: monthly solar energy, NASA POWER -->
            <figure class="my-10">
