                    <line x1="48" x2="712" y1="134.9" y2="134.9" stroke="#e5e7eb" stroke-width="1" /><text x="40" y="138.9" text-anchor="end" class="chart-axis">4</text>
                    <line x1="48" x2="712" y1="203.4" y2="203.4" stroke="#e5e7eb" stroke-width="1" /><text x="40" y="207.4" text-anchor="end" class="chart-axis">2</text>
                    <line x1="48" x2="712" y1="272.0" y2="272.0" stroke="#d1d5db" stroke-width="1" /><text x="40" y="276.0" text-anchor="end" class="chart-axis">0</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>January: 1.03 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="48.0" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M63.7,272 V240.7 Q63.7,236.7 67.7,236.7 H83.7 Q87.7,236.7 87.7,240.7 V272 Z" fill="#1e40af" /></g>
                    <text x="75.7" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Jan</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>February: 1.92 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="103.3" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M119.0,272 V210.2 Q119.0,206.2 123.0,206.2 H139.0 Q143.0,206.2 143.0,210.2 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="131.0" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Feb</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>March: 2.85 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="158.7" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M174.3,272 V178.3 Q174.3,174.3 178.3,174.3 H194.3 Q198.3,174.3 198.3,178.3 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="186.3" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Mar</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>April: 4.15 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="214.0" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M229.7,272 V133.7 Q229.7,129.7 233.7,129.7 H249.7 Q253.7,129.7 253.7,133.7 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="241.7" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Apr</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>May: 5.25 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="269.3" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M285.0,272 V96.0 Q285.0,92.0 289.0,92.0 H305.0 Q309.0,92.0 309.0,96.0 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="297.0" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">May</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>June: 5.73 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="324.7" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M340.3,272 V79.5 Q340.3,75.5 344.3,75.5 H360.3 Q364.3,75.5 364.3,79.5 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="352.3" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Jun</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>July: 6.33 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="380.0" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M395.7,272 V59.0 Q395.7,55.0 399.7,55.0 H415.7 Q419.7,55.0 419.7,59.0 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="407.7" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Jul</text>
                    <text x="407.7" y="47.0" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-value">6.33</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>August: 5.40 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="435.3" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M451.0,272 V90.9 Q451.0,86.9 455.0,86.9 H471.0 Q475.0,86.9 475.0,90.9 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="463.0" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Aug</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>September: 3.91 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="490.7" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M506.3,272 V141.9 Q506.3,137.9 510.3,137.9 H526.3 Q530.3,137.9 530.3,141.9 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="518.3" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Sep</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>October: 2.28 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="546.0" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M561.7,272 V197.8 Q561.7,193.8 565.7,193.8 H581.7 Q585.7,193.8 585.7,197.8 V272 Z" fill="#3b82f6" /></g>
                    <text x="573.7" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Oct</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>November: 1.22 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="601.3" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M617.0,272 V234.2 Q617.0,230.2 621.0,230.2 H637.0 Q641.0,230.2 641.0,234.2 V272 Z" fill="#1e40af" /></g>
                    <text x="629.0" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Nov</text>
                    <g class="bar"><title>December: 0.85 kWh per square meter per day</title><rect x="656.7" y="32" width="55.3" height="240" fill="transparent" /><path d="M672.3,272 V246.9 Q672.3,242.9 676.3,242.9 H692.3 Q696.3,242.9 696.3,246.9 V272 Z" fill="#1e40af" /></g>
                    <text x="684.3" y="292" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-axis">Dec</text>
                    <text x="684.3" y="234.9" text-anchor="middle" class="chart-value">0.85</text>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <details class="mt-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
                  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">Show the numbers</summary>
                  <table class="w-full mt-3 border-collapse">
                    <caption class="sr-only">Average daily sunlight on flat ground near Seattle by month, kWh per square meter per day, 2001-2020</caption>
                    <thead>
                      <tr class="border-b border-gray-300">
                        <th class="text-left py-1 pr-4">Month</th>
                        <th class="text-right py-1">kWh per square meter per day</th>
                      </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                      {solarByMonth.map((row) => (
                        <tr class="border-b border-gray-100">
                          <td class="py-1 pr-4">{row.month}</td>
                          <td class="py-1 text-right tabular-nums">{row.value}</td>
                        </tr>
                      ))}
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </details>
              </div>
              <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2 italic text-center">
                Source: NASA POWER, all-sky surface shortwave climatology for the Seattle area, 2001-2020. Tilted panels catch more than flat ground in winter, but the shape of the year doesn't change.
              </figcaption>
            </figure>

            <p>
              It's wet, too. NOAA's 1991-2020 <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/access/us-climate-normals/#dataset=normals-monthly&timeframe=30&station=USW00024233" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">climate normals for Sea-Tac Airport</a> come to 39.34 inches of precipitation a year, about 75% of it falling October through March. A typical December brings measurable precipitation on 18 of its 31 days. <a href="https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/access/us-climate-normals/#dataset=normals-monthly&timeframe=30&station=USW00024227" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Olympia Airport</a> is wetter: 50.62 inches a year, 78% of it October through March, and about 20 days with measurable precipitation in a typical December.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              This winter may lean drier than that. NOAA's <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso_advisory/ensodisc.shtml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Climate Prediction Center</a> said on October 8 that a strong to very strong El Niño is likely through January-March 2027. Deputy State Climatologist Karin Bumbaco told the <a href="https://environment.uw.edu/news/2026/08/what-to-expect-from-a-very-strong-el-nino-in-the-pacific-northwest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">UW College of the Environment</a> in August that El Niño years usually bring "lower precipitation than normal, particularly west of the Cascades," and added, "We're definitely not guaranteed to see the same thing this winter." A drier winter would help a solar panel a little. It does nothing for the day length or the angle of the sun, and those two set the ceiling.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 mt-12">2. What happens on the fifth gray day in a row</h2>

            <p>
              A solar trailer runs like a bank account. The panels are the income and the battery is the balance. The cameras, infrared lights, recorder and cellular modem are the spending, and they spend around the clock. In July the income beats the spending and the battery refills every day. In December, on a lot of days, it doesn't, so the balance drops a little each day until the sun comes back or something else adds to it.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              Here's a worked example with round, hypothetical numbers, not a spec for our trailers or anyone else's. Say the equipment draws an average of 100 watts, day and night. That's 2.4 kWh a day. Give it 1,000 watts of panels. On NASA's average December day of 0.85 kWh per square meter on flat ground, those panels collect a bit under 1 kWh before wiring and charging losses. Even if tilting them toward the south doubled that, it still wouldn't cover 2.4 kWh. So the battery makes up the difference, about 1.5 kWh on an average December day and more on a dark one. With 10 kWh of usable battery, that's roughly six average December days from full, and fewer if the stretch is darker than average. In July the same panels collect about 6.3 kWh a day, more than twice what the equipment uses.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              Long nights add to the load. On December 21 there are more than 15 and a half hours between sunset and sunrise in Seattle, and infrared night vision runs the whole time. Winter is when the cameras work hardest and the panels work least.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              Cold adds a less obvious problem. If the battery bank is lithium iron phosphate (LiFePO4), a common choice for off-grid solar, it shouldn't be charged when it's too cold. <a href="https://battlebornbatteries.com/blogs/academy/understanding-temperature-limits-of-lifepo4-batteries-the-battle-born-educational-series" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Battle Born Batteries</a> says its LiFePO4 batteries shouldn't be charged below about 25 degrees F unless they have internal heating, and that the battery management system blocks charging outside safe limits. Victron's Smart lithium batteries have a higher floor: a <a href="https://www.victronenergy.com/blog/2018/11/28/victronconnect-now-with-smart-lithium-update/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">2018 Victron update</a> blocks charging below 5 degrees C (41 degrees F). Discharging is fine at much lower temperatures, down to about -4 degrees F by Battle Born's figure, so the cameras keep running. What you lose is charging time.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              That matters more the farther south you go on I-5. NOAA's normals give Olympia 77 nights a year at or below freezing and a normal December low of 32.8 degrees F. Sea-Tac averages 25 freezing nights a year, and its December normal low of 37.1 degrees is still under Victron's 41-degree floor. On a frosty Olympia morning, an unheated battery in a cold enclosure may not take a charge until it warms up, and in December the useful sun only lasts a few hours.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              None of this means solar doesn't work here. It means a winter-ready setup has backup charging that doesn't depend on the sun, usually a generator, or shore power (a plug-in to the site's electrical service) where there is one. It also needs someone who can see the battery level before it turns into a problem.
            </p>

            <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
              <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-3">Questions to ask before a solar camera goes on a winter site</p>
              <p class="text-blue-900 mb-3">Ask these of any vendor, us included.</p>
              <ul class="pl-6 space-y-3 text-blue-900" style="list-style-type: '- ';">
                <li>How many days does this exact configuration run with no sun at its December load, with infrared on all night?</li>
                <li>What charges the battery when the panels can't: a generator, shore power or nothing? Does the generator start on its own, and how often does it need fuel?</li>
                <li>Who watches the battery level, and what happens when it gets low?</li>
                <li>Is the battery heated or insulated, and what does it do on a 25-degree morning?</li>
                <li>When power gets tight, what shuts off first, and does recording stay on?</li>
                <li>How long does the recorder keep footage, and how do I export a clip?</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 mt-12">3. Power out, cell network down: what keeps recording</h2>

            <p>
              When people ask whether a camera "works," they usually mean three things: it's recording, you can watch it live, and it tells you when something happens. Each of those depends on different pieces, and in a storm they fail separately.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              Recording happens on the trailer. As long as the trailer has power, the recorder keeps writing video, with or without a cellular connection. Live view needs the trailer, the cellular network and your phone all working. Alerts need all of that plus the service that sends them.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              A trailer on its own solar and battery doesn't care whether the grid is up. Cameras plugged into a jobsite's temporary power do, unless they have their own battery backup. The November 2024 bomb cyclone is the reference case around here. Outages across the region topped 640,000 on the night of November 19, <a href="https://www.kuow.org/stories/2-killed-more-than-600-000-without-power-western-washington-bomb-cyclone" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">KUOW reported</a>, and three days later Puget Sound Energy still had more than 166,000 customers affected (<a href="https://www.knkx.org/environment/2024-11-22/seattle-washingon-wind-storm-weather-november-power-outages" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">KNKX, from the Associated Press</a>).
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              The cellular network is a different story, because cell sites need power too. During that storm, <a href="https://www.verizon.com/about/news/verizon-relief-pacific-northwest-storm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Verizon said</a> "massive refueling operations continue to ensure those sites without commercial power remain in service." That's the carriers doing their job, and a reminder that part of the network was running on generators. If the nearest cell site goes down or gets overloaded, a solar trailer keeps recording, but live view and alerts wait until the connection comes back. A trailer that can fail over between cellular networks narrows that gap.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              Two habits follow from this. After any storm, check the trailer and pull the clips you need instead of assuming an alert would have told you. And know your recorder's retention, because footage from a cell outage only helps if you get to it before the archive loops over it.
            </p>

