          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 mt-12">4. Where to park a solar trailer for a Puget Sound winter</h2>

            <p>
              Placement is the part you control. A spot that looked sunny on an August walk-through can sit in shade all day in December.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              The geometry explains it. With the sun about 19 degrees up at noon on December 21, anything on the south side of the trailer casts a shadow about three times its height. A 100-foot Douglas fir throws a shadow around 290 feet long at noon. At 9 a.m., with the sun about 7 degrees up in the southeast, that shadow runs well over 700 feet. At noon in June, the same tree's shadow is about 45 feet. (Those lengths come from the Naval Observatory's sun positions for Seattle and simple trigonometry.)
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              So look south. Stand where you plan to park and check what's between you and the southern sky: firs, the building next door, stacked materials, and the building you're putting up. A four-story frame on the south side of the lot in October can have the trailer in its shadow by January.
            </p>

            <!-- Supporting image: long fir shadows across a jobsite -->
            <figure class="my-10">
              <LazyImage src="/images/solar-winter-fir-shadows.webp"
                alt="A muddy construction site in Western Washington with a low winter sun shining through a row of tall Douglas firs, throwing long shadows across the wet gravel toward a skid steer and stacked materials"
                class="w-full rounded-lg shadow-lg" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" />
              <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-600 mt-2 italic text-center">
                Low winter sun through a stand of firs. Shadows like these sweep across a site for hours on a December day, and a panel sitting in one gets only a fraction of the light.
              </figcaption>
            </figure>

            <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">The rest of the winter setup</h3>

            <ul class="pl-6 space-y-3 mb-6" style="list-style-type: '- ';">
              <li><strong>Firm, drained ground.</strong> A trailer on soft fill or in a low spot can settle or end up standing in water. Put it on gravel or pavement, level, somewhere a truck can still reach after a week of rain.</li>
              <li><strong>Access for fuel and service.</strong> If the trailer has a generator, somebody has to get to it. Don't let material deliveries box it in.</li>
              <li><strong>Wind and trees.</strong> The September 25 storm brought a 54 mph gust to Olympia, and meteorologist Scott Sistek noted in his <a href="https://emeraldcityweather.com/northwest-windstorm-101-mph-gust-mount-rainier-seattle-weather/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Emerald City Weather write-up</a> that with trees still in leaf, it took less wind than usual to knock out power. Keep the trailer out from under trees that could come down, and follow the manufacturer's wind guidance for mast height.</li>
              <li><strong>Clean panels.</strong> Rain rinses them, but fir needles, moss and mud thrown up by equipment stay put. Add the panels to the weekly site walk. Snow is less common in the lowlands, with normals of 6.3 inches a year at Sea-Tac and 3.9 at Olympia, but a covered panel produces almost nothing until the snow slides off or gets brushed off.</li>
              <li><strong>Camera views.</strong> Rain beads on a dome and blurs the picture. Low winter sun shines straight into cameras facing southeast in the morning and southwest in the afternoon. Site lights flare in the rain. And a spider web across a lens lights up under infrared and can set off motion alerts all night. Aim with that in mind, and wipe the domes when you clean the panels.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 mt-12">5. A winter checklist for Tacoma, Seattle and Olympia jobsites</h2>

            <p>
              Power is only part of it. Here's the rest of a winter list, short enough to actually use.
            </p>

            <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-6">
              <ul class="pl-6 space-y-3 text-blue-900" style="list-style-type: '- ';">
                <li><strong>Gates and fence.</strong> Chain and lock every gate at the end of the day, including the one the last sub uses. Walk the fence line after every windstorm, because a tree across the fence works like an open gate.</li>
                <li><strong>Materials.</strong> Lock up wire, tools and small equipment, or stage them where a camera can see them. Our <a href="/blog/copper-theft-seattle-tacoma-construction-sites" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">copper theft guide</a> covers what thieves go after on Puget Sound jobsites.</li>
                <li><strong>Lighting.</strong> Put site lights on a photocell instead of a timer set in September. From November 1 through most of January the sun sets before 5 p.m., and a timer that still thinks it's summer leaves the first hours of the night dark.</li>
                <li><strong>Equipment.</strong> Park machines inside the fence and inside a camera's view, take the keys, and disable what you can.</li>
                <li><strong>After-hours contacts.</strong> Decide who gets the alerts, who calls 911 and who drives out, and write down the backups for holiday weeks. Our <a href="/blog/holiday-construction-site-theft-prevention-guide" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">holiday construction site security guide</a> covers the Thanksgiving-to-New-Year stretch.</li>
                <li><strong>Storm warnings.</strong> When a wind or winter storm warning goes out, check the trailer's battery and fuel the day before, not the morning after.</li>
                <li><strong>Night views.</strong> Once a month as the days shorten, look at each camera's night picture and re-aim anything that has lost its view to a new wall or a stack of materials.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6 mt-12">6. Where cameras help on a winter site, and where they don't</h2>

            <p>
              Winter stretches the hours nobody is on site. From mid-November into the first days of January, a crew that leaves at 4:30 walks out after sunset, and nobody is back until morning. Cameras cover exactly those hours. They tell you when someone is inside the fence, give police a call backed by live video, and leave a record for the insurance claim. Our <a href="/blog/washington-security-camera-laws-audio-license-plate-readers-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Washington security camera laws guide</a> explains how Seattle and Tacoma police treat video-verified calls, and why audio recording needs care.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              They don't lock a gate, and they won't stop someone determined who figures nobody will show up in time. Heavy rain and fog cut how far they can see. And a camera on a dead battery records nothing, which is why the first four sections of this guide are about power.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              Our trailers run on solar with a generator for backup, so they don't need site power. Each one carries 4 cameras: 1 fixed fisheye and 3 PTZ cameras with 25x optical zoom and infrared. They connect over 4G/5G with automatic failover, and you monitor them yourself, 24/7, from your phone or computer; there's no staffed monitoring center. The recorder on the trailer keeps two weeks of motion-only footage by default, extendable in special cases. Equipment training and 24/7 technical support come with every rental, and professional delivery and setup are available. How a trailer handles December depends on the site, the camera settings and the weather, so tell us where the job is and how long it runs, and we'll talk through how the power setup will cope. Our <a href="/trailer-standalone-hybrid-power-architecture" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">power system page</a> shows how solar, battery and generator fit together.
            </p>
            <br>

            <p>
              Pricing depends on rental length, site and configuration. Call <a href="tel:2536832288" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">(253) 683-2288</a> for a quote, or see <a href="/pricing" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">rental options &amp; quotes</a>.
            </p>

